// webui-entry — the canonical shape for a revl frontend behind a typed boundary
// (roadmap item 459, issue #722; design docs/design/526-webui-asset-alignment.md
// and docs/design/530-webui-entry-surface.md).
//
// THE ANTI-PATTERN THIS REPLACES. The legacy console is ~4,800 lines of
// HTML/CSS/JS carried inside revl source strings, with browser tooling
// extracting the scripts back out. There are no source maps to an original, the
// frontend cannot be type-checked or bundled by normal tooling, and the set of
// server methods the page can call is not stated anywhere.
//
// THE SHAPE. A revl component contributes a frontend by naming EXTERNAL asset
// files and handing them to Cordis WebUI (`ctx.webui.addEntry`, studied in
// design note 526). WebUI is an AMBIENT SERVICE the host runtime provides: the
// component declares a first-class coeffect on it — `requires webui: WebUI` —
// and reaches it through that typed boundary. No JavaScript lives in a revl
// string; the frontend entry itself (`frontend/entry.client.ts`) is a normal
// Vite/Vue source with source maps, named here only by PATH.
//
// WHY A COEFFECT, NOT A `globalThis` BRIDGE. The enabling slice (#761) reached
// the Cordis `Context` through the retained embedder bridge
// `globalThis.__revlWebui`, a `@ts ref` host-ref door onto an untyped seam.
// Design note 530 flagged that as a slice compromise and asked the architect to
// choose the first-class surface; the coeffect (option 1) was approved. This is
// that surface: `webui` is a declared `requires` key, so the emitted artifact
// resolves it through Cordis' own `inject` (the host-provided `ctx.webui`), and
// the whole frontend-entry contribution — the coeffect and its `add_entry`
// emission — is an ENUMERABLE crossing `revl audit` (G1) reports and G8 marks as
// the trusted host boundary. There is no `globalThis` reach and no untyped door.
//
// This is the ENABLING SLICE, not the full close. Two pieces stay app-gated and
// are recorded in design note 530: the typed reactive-state / RPC contract that
// replaces Cordis' untyped `data: T` (folded into item 457's "one definition"
// work — this slice publishes an empty `data` surface deliberately), and the
// exemplary frontend itself, which is item 462's deliverable.
//
// Copy this file the way router.rvl is copied: rename the component, point the
// paths at your own Vite project's dev source, built manifest, and routes.

// The ambient WebUI service — the typed contract the host runtime provides as
// `ctx.webui`. `add_entry` names external asset files and returns a stable entry
// handle; it is an `emission` because registering a frontend is an outward
// effect on the host (G4 upper bound, the shape a `revl import cordis` of
// `@cordisjs/plugin-webui` recovers). A real console would grow this service
// with the typed reactive-state / RPC methods design note 530 defers to 457.
service WebUI {
  emission fn add_entry(
    dev_source: Str,
    prod_manifest: Str,
    routes: List[Str],
  ) -> Str
}

// The console component. It declares a first-class coeffect on the ambient WebUI
// service (`requires webui: WebUI`) and, at activation, registers its frontend
// entry by naming external files only. The binding is the declared coeffect —
// not a `globalThis` bridge and not an inline JS body — so `revl audit` sees the
// `webui` requirement and the `webui.add_entry` emission on this component's
// boundary.
component ConsoleUI requires webui: WebUI {
  emit webui.add_entry(
    "./frontend/entry.client.ts",       // dev-mode source (a Vite/Vue module)
    "./dist/.vite/manifest.json",       // built Vite manifest for production
    ["/console"],                       // client route patterns this entry owns
  )
}
